  "full_text": "Hello and welcome back to JD Science. Today we are exploring reproduction. Human egg and sperm cells each have 23 chromosomes. When they fuse, the zygote has 46 chromosomes, which are 23 pairs. Mitosis makes identical cells for growth and repair. Myosis makes gametes with half the chromosomes and each gamete is genetically different. Sexual reproduction needs two parents and produces variation. Asexual reproduction needs one parent and produces clones. Genes are sections of DNA on chromosomes and each gene codes for a protein. DNA is a double helix made of nucleotides and bases pair as C with G and A with T. For genetics, each parent passes on one allele of each gene. Dominant alleles show up even when only one copy is present. Recessive alleles only show when two copies are present. Cystic fibrosis is recessive. Polydactyly is dominant. Sex is determined by the X and Y chromosomes. Myosis reduces chromosome number and generates genetic variation through processes including recombination and independent assortment. Fertilization restores the diploid chromosome number. In inheritance questions, always define the allele symbols before completing a Punnett square. A dominant allele is expressed in a heterozygote. This does not mean it is necessarily more common in the population. As you revise this topic, try to explain the key process in your own words. Focus on what happens, why it happens, and how the structures involved help the process work. Now pause the video and try this exam style question. A woman is heterozygous for cystic fibrosis, F, and a man does not carry the allele FF. Using a Punnett square, work out the probability that their child has cystic fibrosis. Use F for the recessive allele. Here is a model answer. ParentsFFF gives offspring FFFFFF. The child has cystic fibrosis only if FF. Probability equals sign 0%. If this explanation has helped, please subscribe to JD Science for more lessons. You can find further learning resources and practice questions at jdscience.co.uk. Thank you for watching.",
